    "case_summary": "A 66-year-old female patient with a history of Hashimoto's thyroiditis and thrombocytopenia. The patient had consistently shown leukocytopenia and thrombocytopenia in routine blood tests over the years. Initial blood test revealed a leukocyte count of 3.16 × 10^9/L and a platelet count of 109.00 × 10^9/L. No other symptoms were present, and no significant abnormalities were detected in cardiac enzymes or blood coagulation. Four hours post-procedure, the patient experienced intermittent abdominal pain and occasional nausea. Nine hours following the operation, the patient experienced recurrent right abdomen pain progressing to involve the entire abdomen, with persistent nausea and perceived increase in body temperature.\n\nInitial examination showed tenderness in the right abdomen, without rebound tenderness and muscular tension, with normal bowel sounds. Later examination revealed diffuse tenderness with rebound tenderness, primarily in the right abdomen. Dullness to percussion was observed, while pain during percussion in the liver area was absent. Shifting dullness to percussion was negative, and normal bowel sounds were detected. Maximum body temperature was 37.8°C.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  - WBC count: 3.43 × 10^9/L\n  - Neutrophil percentage: 85.40%\n  - Lymphocyte percentage: 9.40%\n  - Absolute lymphocytes: 0.32 × 10^9/L\n  - Platelet count: 79.00 × 10^9/L\n  - CRP level: 2.45 mg/L\n  - Procalcitonin level: 0.58 ng/ml\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  CT Scan Results: Edematous thickening of the intestinal wall in the ascending colon with peripheral exudative changes. No signs of intestinal perforation or obstruction.\n  Follow-up CT: Distension of anterior wall of ascending colon with gas, exudation in subserosa of middle section, and small quantity of ascites.\n\n- Bacterial Culture:\n  Ascites culture yielded growth of Escherichia coli.\n\n- Histological Examination:\n  Showed edema in all layers of intestinal wall, predominant thickening and edema in submucosa, diffuse infiltration of neutrophils with presence of abscesses. Bacterial colonies observed in submucosa surrounding the ESD lesion.",
